<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Argyll</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Argyll is a region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, made up of sea lochs, peninsulas and islands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintyre">Kintyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowal">Cowal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorn">Lorn</a>. Its name means 'coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a>'. It was the British territory of the early medieval kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> and later the base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerled">Somerled</a>'s Kingdom of the Isles and of the Campbell earls and dukes of Argyll.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/argyll.jpg" alt="Argyll" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: OwenBlacker, updated by Elianfoo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Argyllshire_locator_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dál Riata was a Gaelic kingdom that straddled the North Channel, with lands in the north-east of what is now County Antrim and, across the sea, in <b>Argyll</b> and the islands of the Inner Hebrides.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Scotland kept its own courts, law and church, and its administration was largely left to political managers such as the Duke of <b>Argyll</b> and later Henry Dundas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Scotland grew out of the union of the Picts, who held most of the land north of the Forth, and the Gaelic kingdom of Dál Riata in <b>Argyll</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A smaller government army under the Duke of <b>Argyll</b> fought the Jacobite army of the Earl of Mar near Dunblane in Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 7th century it controlled Lorn, Mull and neighbouring districts in northern <b>Argyll</b>, with its main stronghold at Dunollie near Oban.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-loairn">Cenel Loairn</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 places the Epidii in the Kintyre peninsula and neighbouring islands of <b>Argyll</b>, with the Mull of Kintyre named Epidion Akron after them.</blockquote><a href="/state/epidii">Epidii</a></li></ul></section>
